{% doc %}
  Every variant option of a product, each with a control suited to it.

  DATA-DRIVEN, NOT A LIST OF KNOWN OPTIONS. It walks product.options_with_values,
  so a merchant adding "Material" — or a fourth size — gets a working control
  with no code change. Nothing here names "Size".

  Colour keeps the circles (they are its whole language); every other option
  gets buttons or a dropdown, chosen by the block's setting. Circles are
  deliberately NOT reused for non-colour options: a circle communicates a
  colour and nothing else.

  HOW A VALUE KNOWS WHERE IT GOES: Shopify's `option_value.variant` is the
  variant you land on by changing THIS option while keeping the others as they
  are. That is what makes multi-option products resolve correctly without the
  theme doing its own combinatorics, and it is the same resolution colour has
  always used.

  NO JS REQUIRED. Every choice is a link to that variant's own URL, so a click
  navigates and Shopify renders the page with it selected. theme.js upgrades
  the same elements into in-place updates. The dropdown form additionally
  renders the plain link list for the no-JS case, since a <select> alone can
  do nothing without script.

  @param {product} product
  @param {string} [style] - "buttons" (default) or "dropdown", for non-colour
    options only
  @param {string} [id_prefix] - unique prefix for label/aria ids
  @example
  {% render 'variant-options', product: product, style: block.settings.option_style %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
  {%- assign control_style = style | default: 'buttons' -%}
  {%- assign prefix = id_prefix | default: 'pdp' -%}

  {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign option_key = option.name | downcase
      assign is_colour = false
      if option_key == 'color' or option_key == 'colour'
        assign is_colour = true
      endif
      comment
        Also treat it as colour when Shopify itself hands us swatches for it —
        a merchant may name the option "Shade" and still have it connected to
        the colour taxonomy.
      endcomment
      unless is_colour
        for value in option.values
          if value.swatch
            assign is_colour = true
            break
          endif
        endfor
      endunless
    -%}

    {%- if is_colour -%}
      {% render 'color-swatches', product: product, context: 'pdp' %}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- assign group_id = prefix | append: '-opt-' | append: forloop.index -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Named ...-group, not data-variant-option: the swatches already use
        data-variant-option for the VALUE's name, and one attribute meaning
        two different things is a trap for the next selector written.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="variant-option" data-variant-option-group>
        <p class="variant-option__label" id="{{ group_id }}">{{ option.name }}</p>

        {%- if control_style == 'dropdown' -%}
          <div class="variant-option__select-wrap">
            <select
              class="variant-option__select"
              aria-labelledby="{{ group_id }}"
              data-variant-nav
            >
              {%- for value in option.values -%}
                <option
                  value="{{ value.variant.url }}"
                  data-variant-id="{{ value.variant.id }}"
                  data-option-position="{{ option.position }}"
                  data-option-value="{{ value.name | escape }}"
                  {% if value.selected %}
                    selected
                  {% endif %}
                  {% unless value.available %}
                    disabled
                  {% endunless %}
                >
                  {{ value.name }}
                  {%- unless value.available %} — {{ 'products.sold_out' | t }}{% endunless -%}
                </option>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </select>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The link list is the button UI, and also the no-JS fallback for the
          dropdown — CSS hides it in that case only when scripting is on.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div
          class="variant-option__list{% if control_style == 'dropdown' %} variant-option__list--fallback{% endif %}"
          role="group"
          aria-labelledby="{{ group_id }}"
        >
          {%- for value in option.values -%}
            {%- assign target = value.variant -%}
            {%- assign value_image = target.featured_media | default: target.image -%}
            <a
              class="variant-option__button{% unless value.available %} variant-option__button--unavailable{% endunless %}"
              href="{{ target.url }}"
              data-variant-control
              data-option-position="{{ option.position }}"
              data-option-value="{{ value.name | escape }}"
              data-variant-id="{{ target.id }}"
              data-variant-available="{{ target.available }}"
              data-variant-price="{{ target.price | money | escape }}"
              {% if target.compare_at_price > target.price %}
                data-variant-compare="{{ target.compare_at_price | money | escape }}"
              {% endif %}
              {% if value_image %}
                data-variant-image="{{ value_image | image_url: width: 1500 }}"
                data-variant-image-alt="{{ value_image.alt | escape }}"
              {% endif %}
              {% if value.selected %}
                aria-current="true"
              {% endif %}
            >
              {{ value.name }}
            </a>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endunless -%}
